grity  a recent report published by CP HL  highlights the pharmaceutical industry  drive to improve productivity by  improving right first-time quality thus  reducing manufacturing costs  another report states that 79% of all  483 warning letters issued by the USA  FDA in 2016  cited 21 CFR part 11 data integrity  issues whilst Retraining is a common  response to an FDA 483 warning letter  this may not this may be only part of  the solution was the problem simply that  the technicians didn't follow the SOP  correctly was it really that the manual  SOPs are simply not robust enough to  prevent future recurrences of the same  problem any process that automates the  quality control SOP can potentially both  reduce the impact of human error on data  integrity issues and also bring welcome  savings by reducing the time technicians  need to devote to carrying out the SOP  despite encouragement from the FDA in  their 2004 process analytical  technologies initiative many routine  quality control test procedures in the  pharmaceutical industry remain intensely  manual and time-consuming in the spirit  of EFT of the FDA's p80 initiative users  could consider moving from sole reliance  on laboratory based QC testing to using  the online QC instrumentation instead  for example many companies have  validated online water quality testing  instrumentation on their purified water  and water for injection loops but they  continue to rely on time-consuming  operatory water quality instrumentation  for product release testing as long as  the online instruments can be validated  and meet the requirements the  pharmacopoeias it makes sense to at  least reduce the amount of laboratory  testing and to rely on data from the  online instruments instead as the online  instruments are automated they're used  to make the testing more robust by  avoiding human error and thus improving  data and data integrity and at the same  time reducing QC department costs  a study by the industrial biodiversity  more than 20% variability in test  results between 16 experienced QC  technicians performing the same manual  standard operating procedure now clearly  the manual SOP could be improved and the  technicians retrained but such  variability in results suggests that the  manual process itself is just not robust  enough to deliver the required level of  quality control whilst the technical  specifications of similar QC  instrumentation may appear equivalent  when looking to improve quality control  QC teams should be looking at the level  of automation in the instrument to make  UC testing more robust and repeatable  for example a common source of errors in  QC testing is sample preparation an  instrument that automates the sample  preparation process can bring enhanced  reproducibility and robustness to a QC  test  so let's take a look at our first QC  test today quality control testing for  purified water and water for injection  total organic carbon and conductivity  are to the four critical quality  attributes defined for water injection  and purified water in the United States  Pharmacopeia online analyzers such as  the P 8700 from Beckman Coulter can be  hard data to comply fully for both of  these key quality attributes according  to the farmer kapiel requirements  in their guidance the FDL FDL keen to  emphasize the 21 CFR part 11 ruling  applies only to the date historian where  the electronic records are kept the  danger with online instrumentation with  their own local data historian built-in  is that they could attract the full  requirements of the 21 CFR part 11  ruling analyzes such as the pata 700  avoid this issue by allowing the local  data is storing to be disabled thus  ensuring that it does not attract before  21 CFR part 11 requirements as a data  archive for electronic records now  traditionally data from online toc  analyzer is stored in a validated  supervisory control and data acquisition  or starter system or a distributed  control system 